title: 'statement-store: avoid blocking event loop during initial sync'
doc:
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    Initial-sync chunks are queued as futures driven by the protocol's event loop instead of being
    awaited inline, so an unresponsive peer stalls only its own sync rather than every other
    responsibility of the handler. Statements are marked known to a peer only once its send
    succeeds, at most one chunk per peer is in flight, and a result belonging to a sync that a
    reconnect has since replaced is discarded instead of applied to the new one.
- audience: Node Operator
  description: |-
    `substrate_sync_propagated_statements_chunks` gains a `kind` label (`propagation`,
    `initial_sync`), since initial sync fills every chunk to the size limit while propagation does
    not. Queries that did not aggregate over labels now return one series per kind.

    New `substrate_sync_initial_sync_in_flight_bytes` (gauge) and
    `substrate_sync_initial_sync_bursts_throttled_total` (counter) report use of the new
    `MAX_INITIAL_SYNC_IN_FLIGHT_BYTES` budget, which bounds the encoded chunks initial sync holds
    while their sends are outstanding. `substrate_sync_statement_send_failures_total` and
    `substrate_sync_statement_undelivered_total` can now report `reason="no_sink"` for initial sync,
    which they could not before.
crates:
- name: sc-network-statement
  bump: minor
